      "modelAnswer": "British imperial rule over colonial India—including a British monopoly on salt and economic policies that harmed Indian industries—led the nationalist leader Gandhi, who believed in nonviolent civil disobedience (satyagraha), to defy British law with acts like the Salt March to gain support for Indian independence.",
      "explanation": "Full credit (1 point) for a correct historical circumstance behind Gandhi's 1930 actions. Acceptable responses (NYSED rating guide) include: British imperialism in India inspiring civil disobedience; Gandhi as a nationalist leader inspiring resistance; his belief in satyagraha to change British policies; using the press to gain support for independence; India as a British colony fighting for independence; British colonial policies limiting Indian economic development; British policies collapsing Indian industries; British control of/monopoly on salt; resentment of the salt monopoly; British troops occupying India; British control since the Sepoy Mutiny; or his support for nonviolent resistance after returning to India. Not acceptable: 'the police arrived here today,' 'they will wade into the marshes,' 'Gandhi was a Hindu,' 'the partition of India,' 'Gandhi's assassination,' 'the Salt March,' 'laws were breached,' 'resentment,' 'nationalism,' 'Indian independence,' 'because he thought it was right,' or 'taxes on things.'",
